## Data API Builder (DAB) is a cross-platform, open-source engine that requires zero code and a single configuration file.

## It provides a standardized way to perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on data, enabling seamless integration between different systems and applications. 

## develop and deploy a data API for an Azure SQL Database using Azure Static Web Apps

## Install the Visual Studio Code extensions
# Install the Static Web Apps CLI 
npm install -g @azure/static-web-apps-cli

# Install the Data API Builder CLI
dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.DataApiBuilder

# Create an Azure SQL Database and Add your IP address to the firewall rules
# Add Sample Data to the Database
# Navigate to your newly created Azure SQL Database.
# Use the Query editor in the Azure portal to run the following SQL script:
CREATE TABLE [dbo].[Employees]
(
    EmployeeID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    FirstName NVARCHAR(50),
    LastName NVARCHAR(50),
    Department NVARCHAR(50)
);
    
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Employees] VALUES (1,'John', 'Smith', 'Accounting');
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Employees] VALUES (2,'Michelle', 'Sanchez', 'IT');
    
SELECT * FROM [dbo].[Employees];

## Create a basic web app in GitHub
# This is the repo: https://github.com/Promise-Igbo/my-sql-repo.git

# Create an Azure Static Web App using the repo

## Add Data API Builder configuration file
# clone the GitHub repository on vscode and run command to create a new Data API Builder configuration file
swa db init --database-type "mssql"
# This will create a new folder named swa-db-connections and a file named staticwebapp.database.config.json inside that folder.

# Run the following command to add the database entities to the configuration file
dab add "Employees" --source "dbo.Employees" --permissions "anonymous:*" --config "swa-db-connections/staticwebapp.database.config.json"

# Review the contents of the staticwebapp.database.config.json file, commit and push

## Configure the database connection
# On Azure static web app, Under Settings, select Database connection.
# Select Link existing database.

# Database Type:	Azure SQL Database
# Authentication Type:	Connection String
# Username:	your admin user name
# Password:	the password you gave you admin user
# Acknowledgement checkbox:	Checked

# Select Link.

## Test the Data API endpoint
# navigate to the Azure Static Web App, browse URL of the web app, Add /data-api to the end of the URL and press Enter.
# It should display Healthy to indicate that the Data API is working.
# Add /data-api/rest/Employees to the end of the URL and press Enter. You should see the sample data you added to the Azure SQL Database earlier.
